A||N||6||x-thumpr-x||engine cradle removal||||1||
Z||000000||x-thumpr-x||11-23-2001||09:42 PM||||I have a slight problem with my cradle bolt. Seems like rust has gotten the better part (litterally). The nut which is NORMALLY welded to the cradle has rusted away and now spins when I turn the bolt. the car is ready to be hoisted up away from the engine but one bolt is holding the job back. Any ideas on how to remove (by any means) this bolt without damaging the cradle mounts? I already have a method of installing a replacemnet nut inside the cradle. <P>Help please!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!||||reg||1
Z||000001||avengador1||11-23-2001||09:59 PM||||You will have to cut it off and it is a pita. Sorry to say so.Use a cutting wheel and a die grinder to do the least amount of damage.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Avendgador1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||jelly2m8||11-24-2001||12:37 AM||||Do you mean the rear bolt, the nut up inside the frame?<P> If so, a common method is to cut an access hole with a sawz-all or such, pry the metal up out of the way, and use vise grips to hold that nut in place.<P> You dont need to weld the metal back once you put it back in place. You can if you want, but it is more likely to rust if you do. The heat from welding it will burn the paint, and the rust inhibitor coating off the metal<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://users.eastlink.ca/~acook1/pegged%2001.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||watts||11-24-2001||01:03 AM||||The cleanest way (if there is such a thing!) is to use a hole saw.  Follow the imaginary line of the bolt up through the frame.  Now just cleanly cut about a 1.5 - 2" hole just above the bottom of the frame.  The idea is NOT to cut into the bottom of the frame itself...  so shoot for just a hair over it.<P>Now get in there with a pair of needle nose vise grips and hope.<P>After you've yanked the old bolt out, get in there with some rust killer paint and slop it all around inside as best you can.  Do the edges of the new hole as well.  After you put the new nut in there and button it up with your new bolt, pick up an appropriate size plastic plug and pop it in.  <P>Now at least if you need to do it again, you have human access to it!   (let's all say it together in a BIG sarcastic voice...  THAAAAAANKS GM!!!!)<BR><P>------------------<BR>87/88 V6 5spd Notchback Hybrid<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/watts_sig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||jelly2m8||11-24-2001||12:24 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by watts:<BR><B>The  Follow the imaginary line of the bolt up through the frame.  Now just cleanly cut about a 1.5 - 2" hole just above the bottom of the frame.  The idea is NOT to cut into the bottom of the frame itself...  so shoot for just a hair over it.<P><BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P> Yes, I forgot to mention that, good catch watts!<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://users.eastlink.ca/~acook1/pegged%2001.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Eriamjh||11-24-2001||12:48 PM||||POR-15 is an excellent protector on rusty metal.  I've used it for years with no rust ever returning.  <P>It's expensive, though.  About $90 a gallon, or $30 a quart.  You'll probably need about one ounce to do your job.  Too bad you can't buy it that way.<P>Use the rest on the rest of your Fiero.  You'll be glad you did.||||reg||1
Z||000006||x-thumpr-x||11-24-2001||10:17 PM||||Well, I got it removed finally. took me a couple of hours but I slowly cut the head off the bolt with a dremel tool. Took me so long becuase I was using two (paired up) cut-off disks to cut away peices of the head at a time (10 sets later). Took a break and went to Home depot, just wanted some food from the harvyes their and decided to go in and check out other cutters. Found some heavey dutting cut-offs and had the head removed in no time.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||10
